{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Neovim review transport to Pi",
  "description": "Follow one submitted review from Neovim commands and live annotations across the local bridge to a Pi user message.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "description": "### Orientation\n\nThis tour follows one immutable review snapshot from Neovim to Pi. Neovim saves the snapshot and its stable submission ID before transport. The TypeScript bridge authenticates it, serializes delivery, and caches accepted results so a retry after a lost acknowledgement does not send the review twice."
    },
    {
      "file": "plugin/pi_nvim.lua",
      "pattern": "^create_command\\(\"PiSubmit\", function\\(\\)",
      "description": "### Submission command entry\n\n`:PiSubmit` is the user-facing entry point. The command contains no transport logic; it delegates immediately to [`pi_nvim.submit`](./lua/pi_nvim/init.lua), which keeps plugin registration separate from the stateful review workflow. The nearby `<Plug>(PiSubmit)` mapping reaches this same command."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/pi_nvim/annotations.lua",
      "pattern": "^function M\\.add\\(",
      "description": "### Annotations remain live and recoverable\n\nBefore `:PiSubmit`, `:PiAnnotate` stores each comment with a stable ID, project-relative path, range, buffer, and extmark. The extmark follows buffer edits. The controller atomically persists the records by canonical project root, so a Neovim restart can restore the same pending review without storing a bridge token."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/pi_nvim/init.lua",
      "pattern": "^function M\\.submit\\(\\)",
      "description": "### Submission orchestration\n\n`submit` rejects concurrent, sessionless, or empty requests. It atomically saves one immutable snapshot before delivery and records the attempted state before opening the connection. Risky new snapshots open the exact preview first. Retries reuse the same ID and content. Cleanup requires a matching typed acknowledgement and is saved before only the snapshot IDs are cleared."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/pi_nvim/annotations.lua",
      "pattern": "^function M\\.build\\(\\)",
      "description": "### Resolve, compare, and normalize the payload\n\n`build` resolves every extmark's current range and takes source text from the loaded buffer when possible. It compares each excerpt with the fingerprint captured when the comment was written and records modified-buffer, changed-context, or missing-baseline risks. It then enforces excerpt limits, sorts deterministically, and removes editor-only identity from the wire payload."
    },
    {
      "file": "lua/pi_nvim/client.lua",
      "pattern": "^function M\\.request\\(",
      "description": "### One JSON line over TCP\n\nThe client JSON-encodes the complete request, appends a newline, checks the 1 MiB limit, and opens a TCP connection to the host and port from the selected session manifest. It reads exactly one newline-terminated response, bounds and validates it, and schedules the callback back on Neovim's event loop. This strict one-request/one-response framing prevents ambiguous partial or concatenated messages."
    },
    {
      "file": "extensions/nvim-review/bridge.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private accept\\(socket: Socket\\): void \\{",
      "description": "### The bridge enforces framing and lifecycle\n\nThe bridge accepts sockets only in its `running` state, applies a timeout and byte limit, and permits one newline-delimited request per connection. Shutdown changes the state before closing sockets, cancels handlers that have not entered delivery, and drains delivery that already started. Every response is one JSON line."
    },
    {
      "file": "extensions/nvim-review/bridge.ts",
      "pattern": "^  private async handleRequestLine\\(",
      "description": "### Validate, deduplicate, and serialize\n\nThe handler validates the protocol shape, compares the access token in constant time, and requires the exact session and root. For submissions it fingerprints canonical content. A cached ID returns its prior result, reuse with different content is rejected, and a second active delivery gets a typed `busy` result. Only then can prompt construction reach Pi."
    },
    {
      "file": "extensions/nvim-review/bridge.ts",
      "pattern": "^function formatReviewPrompt\\(",
      "description": "### Convert annotations into an actionable prompt\n\nThe bridge formats the validated annotations as one user message: instructions, canonical project root, quoted review comments, file locations, and numbered source excerpts. Keeping formatting after validation means this function can work with trusted `ReviewAnnotation` values, while preserving the reviewer context that Neovim captured."
    },
    {
      "file": "extensions/nvim-review/index.ts",
      "pattern": "onSubmit: \\(prompt, count\\) => \\{",
      "description": "### Deliver now or queue a follow-up\n\nThe bridge's `onSubmit` callback is the final handoff to Pi. If Pi is idle, `pi.sendUserMessage(prompt)` starts processing immediately; if Pi is busy, `deliverAs: \"followUp\"` queues the review. After delivery, the bridge caches the typed `accepted` or `queued` result before it sends the acknowledgement. UI notification failure cannot turn accepted delivery into a retry."
    }
  ]
}
